


She wrote PHONE PLACEMENT — POSSIBLE MATCH TO RADIO CONTACT and underlined possible once.




The file continued.




Jonah’s questions grew slower after the phone was down. Between them came insect noise, isolated leaf movement, and long intervals in which nothing distinct happened. The sound Maren had heard after his question about being wanted back occurred on the phone as a brief rough modulation above the insects. On first playback neither of them spoke.




Maren replayed only that second.




Jonah leaned toward the speaker.




She played it again at the same volume.




“What do you hear?” she asked.




He kept listening past the end. “Maybe ‘go.’”




“I don’t.”




“Play it once more.”




“No. Write your first impression.”




He looked at her, then took the yellow pad she pushed across the desk. He wrote MAYBE GO and dated the line.




Maren wrote SPEECH-LIKE MODULATION / NO IDENTIFIED SYLLABLE on her own sheet.




They moved on.




The phone picked up branches and running footfalls before it caught Maren’s voice: Jonah. Show me your hands. Stand up. For the next several minutes, nearly everything they said was clear because they stayed close to the phone. The sequence was less orderly than Jonah’s closing statement from the shelter. The sounds he had described as one approach were broken up by their own speech and movement, his knee hitting the stone, and the phone sliding across the surface.




Maren marked each sound without naming a source. LEAF CONTACT. BRUSH MOVEMENT. STONE/PHONE CONTACT. LOW IMPACT. She did not use the word footstep.




Jonah watched the labels accumulate.




“You’re cutting all the verbs out of it,” he said.




Maren kept the cursor on the next event.




“You decided what didn’t.”




She pressed play again.




On the recording, his own voice said, It’s coming from both sides.




Maren answered, We do not have a direction.




They listened to the three disturbances again. The first carried a dry scrape. The second was heavier and shorter. Maren remembered the third as coming from the opposite side after both had shifted position, but the phone recording did not provide a reliable bearing. Nothing in the recording joined them into one moving source.




Jonah reached for his pen and wrote THREE MOVEMENTS AROUND STONE.




Maren did not correct his page. On hers she wrote THREE SEPARATE DISTURBANCES / RELATION UNKNOWN.




They left both labels on the table.




They continued through the moment Maren stopped her camera. The phone captured Jonah asking her to turn it back on. It captured her refusing. It captured him saying the movement came when he asked it closer and that she arrived immediately after. Then came the dry disturbance Maren remembered as leaves moving behind the stone. Jonah reached for the phone, and Maren caught his injured hand.




His breath changed when she touched him.




Neither of them commented on it.




Maren listened to herself order him to stand. She heard him say the phone was the only clean recording. She heard her answer that he was bleeding, off trail, and not answering the radio. The phone captured a low impact at the point Maren remembered hearing one from the far side of the stone.




Then their retreat began.




The phone stayed behind.




Maren let the recording run beyond the point where she had stopped it earlier that morning. Their voices faded first. Their footfalls lasted longer. For several seconds the recorder held both: faint movement downhill and the steady insect bed around the stone.




Then the human sounds were gone.




Jonah sat forward again.




Maren raised one hand before he could speak.




The unattended interval began with almost a minute of insects and distant road noise. A frog called far below the rise. Leaves shifted once in a short run and stopped. Another minute passed. The recording captured a cluster of dry contacts, but no body, gait, source, or direction could be recovered from audio alone.




Maren marked the time from file start, not clock time.




At the next low sound, Jonah pointed at the waveform.




“That.”




“I have it.”




The contact was dull and brief. Several seconds later came another, quieter sound that might have been a twig or leaf stem against the phone case. The insects continued through both.




They kept listening.




No voice was audible.




A longer rustle rose unevenly in the recording. They could not get a reliable direction from the audio. A distant vehicle passed. Wind moved through higher leaves for perhaps ten seconds, then settled.




Near the end of the file came three low contacts separated by uneven gaps.




The first barely rose above the noise floor.




The second was clearer.




The third carried a small plastic rattle from the phone case against the stone.




Jonah stopped breathing for the length of it.




The recording continued for another twelve seconds with insects and distant water.




Then it ended.




Neither moved until playback stopped and the speakers were silent.




Jonah pointed to the last three peaks. “Those came closer.”




Maren wrote THIRD CONTACT — CASE RATTLE / SOURCE UNKNOWN.




“The last one moved the phone.”




She underlined SOURCE UNKNOWN.




Jonah kept his finger on the last peak.




Maren saved the log.




He pushed his chair back from the desk.




Neither spoke for several seconds.




They spent the next two hours on the other cards.




All three audio recorders captured the distant cry, but they had not been positioned as a microphone array for direction finding. The trail recorder captured it at a higher level than the shelter unit. Later in the night, the coyotes across the river produced pitch changes and overlapping calls that sounded like more animals than Maren could see. One call rose and broke in a way that made both of them look at each other.




“It isn’t the same,” Jonah said.




“No.”




Maren logged the comparison as support for an animal source without identifying one: DISTANT VOCALIZATION — SOURCE UNRESOLVED / WILDLIFE REMAINS VIABLE.




They had only their field notes and camera commentary for the cold pocket. On the return walk, neither had found it again. The location lay on lower ground near the river where cooler air could collect. The decay odor returned near the depression, weaker on the return, with visible flies and likely animal remains below the trail. Maren retained both events in the chronology and removed them from the anomaly list.




The apparent wildlife-response pattern weakened more quickly under review. Her blue-route control preserved the problem she had felt in the dark: frogs called after a direct question, then again after the statement Camera battery sixty-three percent. Other chorus changes occurred without prompts. Nothing in the sequence required the animals to be answering either of them.




Jonah did not object when she moved that entry from APPARENT RESPONSE to ENVIRONMENTAL TIMING.




The shelter impact remained unidentified. The apparent figure had resolved into the back of a trail sign. One scuff matched cable vibration. Several brush movements had no visible source. Ruth’s daylight account added another ordinary comparison: she had heard something moving in brush near the stone and had never seen what made it.
